  "response": "Distinguishing a drug reaction from viral hepatitis begins with characterising the biochemical pattern of injury, since the relation between aminotransferase and alkaline phosphatase elevation narrows the differential before any cause is assigned. A hepatocellular pattern of liver injury is defined by an R ratio of 5 or greater, and Hy's law describes ALT elevation above three times the upper limit of normal accompanied by total bilirubin above twice the upper limit of normal without significant alkaline phosphatase elevation; the ratio-based definition and the Hy's law criterion cannot both be correct characterisations of the same injury. A further specification circulates in the clinical literature: an R ratio below 2 defines a hepatocellular pattern of liver injury. That cutoff cannot be squared with the requirement of a ratio of 5 or greater, for a single pattern cannot be fixed by two incompatible thresholds.\n\nEven though Hy's law supplies so exact a biochemical signature, pattern recognition on its own assigns no aetiology, and the reasoning that must follow is of an altogether different kind. Because drug-induced liver injury is established as a diagnosis of exclusion, anti-HEV IgM and HEV RNA testing is recommended in patients with suspected drug-induced liver injury. The serological work therefore carries the diagnostic weight.\n\nEach hepatotropic virus has its own marker of recent infection. Detection of IgG antibody to hepatitis A virus confirms acute hepatitis A infection, whereas hepatitis B is identified by a marker of an entirely different class. IgM antibody to hepatitis B core antigen marks acute hepatitis B infection, and HCV RNA testing detects acute hepatitis C infection before anti-HCV antibodies become measurable; an antibody response that itself marks acute infection cannot be squared with viral nucleic acid appearing ahead of any measurable antibody. A panel negative across these agents, in a patient with a plausible exposure, shifts the balance decisively toward injury from a medication.\n\nWhere serology leaves the question open, temporal and histological evidence becomes decisive. The latency between starting a drug and the onset of idiosyncratic liver injury commonly falls between 5 and 90 days. Liver biopsy findings of eosinophilic infiltration and granulomas favour a drug reaction over viral hepatitis. The patient under consideration was taking a drug with recognised hepatotoxic potential at the time the injury began; and whereas the histological features just described favour a drug reaction, this patient also had an active viral infection involving the liver at that time. At least one of the two exposures, the hepatotoxic medication or the hepatic viral infection, must account for the injury, and perhaps both contributed.\n\nThe drug exposure came first and the resolution only afterwards: liver enzyme values returned to normal after the suspected drug was stopped; that is, discontinuation of the medication was followed by normalization of the patient's aminotransferase levels. It is this sequence of exposure, injury, withdrawal and recovery that settles the attribution when the two presentations initially look alike.",
